{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790361398,"nonce":"F28gTIvB9ZKryKFW19iPPopUdaw=","title":"@cooperative: a fiber step inside an async task holds the executor","message":"`f.next()` inside an `async def` resumes the fiber synchronously on the task's stack (`FiberResume` lowers to `krio_fiber_resume`), so the executor runs nothing else until the task itself reaches an `await`. A fiber body cannot `await` at all: `SsaBuilder` refuses it (\"await is not allowed inside a fiber function\"). The `@cooperative` annotation and its `@coop` alias are parsed and recorded (`FunctionAttributes::cooperative`, set in `LoweringContext::set_function_attributes`, crates/compiler/src/lowering.rs), and nothing reads the flag; the krio promise entry does not carry it.\n\nWhat exists: krio classifies `Intrinsic::Await` and `PerformEffect` as suspension sites (`HirAsyncHooks::classify`, crates/passes/krio_adapter/src/lib.rs); the executor parks and re-drives futures through `host_futures::register_future` and `resolve_future`; the fiber step encoding has two tag bits and three tags (`fiber_backend::pack_fiber_step`), leaving one tag free.\n\nDone: in an async function, resuming a fiber whose `fiber def` is `@cooperative` is a suspension site, so the executor interleaves other tasks between fiber steps; a cooperative fiber body may `await`, surfacing a \"pending on a future\" step that `emit_fiber_next` decodes and the executor drives. Fibers without the annotation keep today's behaviour and the await-in-fiber refusal. A test runs two tasks each driving a cooperative fiber and observes their steps interleave.","files":null}]}